      "message": "The rest of this commit message is hard to follow.\n- Using a single, long, paragraph makes the text hard to skim.\n- The relationship between the subtopics of this paragraph, and the goals above is hard to see.\n- The paragraph contains information that isn\u0027t relevant to this commit. (E.g., implementation details of WifiAsyncChannel, and WifiHandler.)\n\nPlease have a look at go/lotl-178, and incorporate some of that advice here. The ideal structure of this commit message might be something like:\n\n   Log API surfaces...\n\n   The following interfaces may be used...\n   - AIDL...\n   - Async...\n   - Handler ...\n\n   We trace these interfaces as follows:\n   - AIDL: manual addition of WifiLog.trace() calls\n   - AsyncChannel:\n     - use of WifiAsyncChannel\n     - addition of trace() calls to replySucceeded() and\n       replyFailed() calls [1]\n   - Handler: use of WifiHandler\n\n   [1] This compensates for the fact that WifiScanningService uses a fully-connected AsyncChannel. In fully-connected mode, replies can\u0027t be logged by WifiAsyncChannel, because the replies aren\u0027t sent using replyToMessage().\n\nKey features of the structure above:\n- easy to skim\n- mapping from problem to solution is clear\n- most important information is at the top, with greater levels of details as we reader further (e.g., the detail about replyToMessage() is at the bottom).",
